\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\ul\b\f0\fs24\'b6 Verse 1\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 1. What means this eager, anxious throng,
\par Which moves with busy haste along,
\par These wondrous gatherings day by day?
\par What means this strange commotion, pray?
\par In accents hushed the throng reply:
\par Jesus of Nazareth passeth by.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 2\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 2. Who is this Jesus? Why should He
\par The city move so mightily?
\par A passing stranger, has He skill
\par To move the multitude at will?
\par Again the stirring tones reply:
\par Jesus of Nazareth passeth by.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 3\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 3. Jesus! 'tis He who once below
\par Man's pathway trod 'mid pain and woe;
\par And burdened ones, where'er He came,
\par Brought out their sick, and deaf, and lame:
\par The blind rejoiced to hear the cry:
\par Jesus of Nazareth passeth by.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 4\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 4. Again, He comes! Fromplace to place
\par His holy footprints we can trace,
\par He pauseth at our threshold-nay,
\par He enters- condescends to stay;
\par Shall we not gladly raise th cry?
\par Jesus of Nazareth passeth by.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 5\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 5. Ho! all ye heavy laden, come,
\par Here's pardon, comfort, rest, and home;
\par Ye wanderers from a Father's face,
\par Return, accept His proffered grace;
\par Ye tempted ones, there's refuge nigh-
\par Jesus of Nazareth passeth by.\f1 
\par 
\par }

